{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPk3jYNKEidh9NxEemFx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't give you this fact sheet. Both web tools — `WebSearch` and `WebFetch` — came back \"permissions not granted,\" so I have no way to verify a single figure or citation. Given that this runs as-is under your byline tonight, writing it from my own recollection would be the worst thing I could do: the specific details you need (exact reduction dates, exact per-word rates) are precisely the details I'd be most likely to get subtly wrong, and any citation I produced without opening the source would be fabricated. A made-up author/title/year that *looks* right is a correction in your paper and a problem for you, not for me.\n\nHere's what I can honestly offer.\n\n**Unlock me and I'll do it properly.** Approve web access and I'll have a verified fact sheet back well before tonight — this is maybe ten minutes of work with search. That's the fastest path.\n\n**In the meantime, leads — not citations.** These are places I'm confident the answer lives, offered so you can verify them yourself. I have *not* opened any of them, so do not print any of them on my say-so:\n\n- **atlantic-cable.com** — Bill Burns's \"History of the Atlantic Cable & Undersea Communications.\" The standard reference; hosts scanned primary documents including company circulars and tariff notices. Most likely to have the exact rate schedule.\n- **The Times (London), late July / early August 1866** — the opening tariff was announced publicly; a primary-source dateline beats any secondary retelling.\n- **Daniel R. Headrick, *The Invisible Weapon: Telecommunications and International Politics, 1851–1945*** — contains tariff tables and is the usual scholarly cite for rate history.\n- **Simone Müller, *Wiring the World*** — recent scholarship on the cable business, including pricing.\n- **John Steele Gordon, *A Thread Across the Ocean*** — popular history; good for narrative, weaker for exact figures.\n- **Anglo-American Telegraph Company annual reports** — where the reductions would be recorded first-hand.\n\n**What I'd flag before you write.** Two things routinely get garbled in secondary accounts, so pin them down at the source: whether the opening rate was a *per-word* price or a *minimum-message* price (a 20-word minimum reads as \"£1 a word\" in some retellings and \"£20 a message\" in others — same tariff, different-looking sentence), and whether address and signature were chargeable words. Also worth separating the company's own reductions from the later price collapse driven by competing cables, which is a different story with different dates.\n\nOne thing I do want to name plainly: I have a rough recollection of the opening figure and the general shape of the reductions, and I've deliberately left it out. Half-remembered numbers are more dangerous to you than no numbers, because they'd anchor your draft and read as confirmed.\n\nSay the word on web access and I'll build the real thing — and I'd suggest publishing it as a page you can hand your editor, with each figure sitting next to its source."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":503,"cache_read_input_tokens":31884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":503},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"794f2cfe-fa45-4e3c-815a-e95aa071a8b9","uuid":"d9cac44c-1cc7-44b3-875f-68f8b91b5e93","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:13:34.504Z","request_id":"req_011CfPk3j8ZfWHU1P3qcRHJX"}
